// 12. mashsplit -- the button-mashing minigame (the uncuff). The
//     accumulator rises 87 per press and drops 15 per FRAME against a
//     threshold of 1000, so at 60 fps it drains twice as fast: stock
//     is won from ~4.5 presses per second and the unfixed patch needs
//     ~10.
//
//     It subtracts half EVERY frame -- 7 and 8, with the value taken
//     from the parameter the script writes, not from a literal -- and
//     a press skips a single decay frame, which is what a press costs
//     a tick in stock. It does not depend on frame parity: a
//     parity-based version suppresses two ticks for a press on an even
//     frame and makes the minigame 12% easier.
//
//     And since the accumulator is what draws the progress bar, moving
//     it every frame is what keeps the bar smooth at 60.
//
//     Verified against stock, sweeping rate AND phase of the injected
//     sequence: 20/20/20 presses to win against 20/20/20 at 8 Hz, and
//     within one press at 5 and 6 Hz, which is stock's own spread.
//
//     0x000F1E10  3C01000F  lui   $at, 0x000F
//     0x000F1E14  8C211E04  lw    $at, 7684($at)
//     0x000F1E18  00000000  nop
//     0x000F1E1C  10200004  beq   $at, $zero, 0x000F1E30
//     0x000F1E20  3C01000F  lui   $at, 0x000F
//     0x000F1E24  AC201E04  sw    $zero, 7684($at)
//     0x000F1E28  10000009  beq   $zero, $zero, 0x000F1E50
//     0x000F1E2C  00000000  nop
//     0x000F1E30  3C010051  lui   $at, 0x0051
//     0x000F1E34  8C21C654  lw    $at, -14764($at)
//     0x000F1E38  00000000  nop
//     0x000F1E3C  30210001  andi  $at, $at, 1
//     0x000F1E40  00240821  addu  $at, $at, $a0
//     0x000F1E44  00010843  sra   $at, $at, 1
//     0x000F1E48  00611823  subu  $v1, $v1, $at
//     0x000F1E4C  A6230040  sh    $v1, 64($s1)
//     0x000F1E50  0809FA24  j     0x0027E890
//     0x000F1E54  00000000  nop
//
patch=1,EE,000F1E10,word,3C01000F
patch=1,EE,000F1E14,word,8C211E04
patch=1,EE,000F1E18,word,00000000
patch=1,EE,000F1E1C,word,10200004
patch=1,EE,000F1E20,word,3C01000F
patch=1,EE,000F1E24,word,AC201E04
patch=1,EE,000F1E28,word,10000009
patch=1,EE,000F1E2C,word,00000000
patch=1,EE,000F1E30,word,3C010051
patch=1,EE,000F1E34,word,8C21C654
patch=1,EE,000F1E38,word,00000000
patch=1,EE,000F1E3C,word,30210001
patch=1,EE,000F1E40,word,00240821
patch=1,EE,000F1E44,word,00010843
patch=1,EE,000F1E48,word,00611823
patch=1,EE,000F1E4C,word,A6230040
patch=1,EE,000F1E50,word,0809FA24
patch=1,EE,000F1E54,word,00000000
//
//     and the press routine, which marks the frame to skip:
//
patch=1,EE,000F1E60,word,3C01000F
patch=1,EE,000F1E64,word,AC211E04
patch=1,EE,000F1E68,word,0200202D
patch=1,EE,000F1E6C,word,0809F9E9
patch=1,EE,000F1E70,word,00000000
patch=0,EE,000F1E04,word,00000000
//
//     The TWO hooks. Neither lands on a branch, so both are one word
//     and their delay slot runs before the routine.
//
patch=1,EE,0027E79C,word,0803C798
patch=1,EE,0027E888,word,0803C784

// 13. lockrate -- the lockpick minigame. The pointer that has to be
//     caught with X advances 0.1 radians PER FRAME, so at 60 fps it
//     goes round in half the wall time -- 1048 ms against 2086 -- and
//     the zone where X hits lasts half as long: 192 ms against 383.
//
//     Both symptoms come from that single fact. The second one -- the
//     first X taking longer to become possible -- is indirect: at the
//     start there is a dead window in which the widget spins but the
//     minigame does not read the pad yet, and with the pointer at
//     double speed the FIRST pass through the zone falls inside it. The
//     X neither hits nor misses, it is lost, and a whole extra turn has
//     to go by. From Triangle to the first X that counts: stock 967 ms,
//     without this section 1535, with it 951.
//
//     The fix is half a step. It is not doubling a threshold -- there
//     is no edge or window to confuse -- but a RATE: half the rate over
//     twice the frames visits the same positions at the same
//     milliseconds, with twice the resolution. And since 0x001B8530
//     rewrites the constant every frame from an immediate, changing
//     the immediate is enough: one word, no cave and no hook.
//
//       lui $at, 0x3DCC   ->   lui $at, 0x3D4C
//       0.1 (3DCCCCCD)          0.05 (3D4CCCCD)
//
patch=1,EE,001B85AC,word,3C013D4C

// 14. brainrate -- the AI round-robin. 0x00293B28 splits the 60
//     brains into groups by index % 5 and fires "think" when the group
//     matches the tick's phase. The rate is calls_per_second / M, and
//     the srl $a0,1 inside does NOT enter that count: it only orders
//     the phases.
//
//     What changes is the numerator. In stock the world update skips
//     odd frames and gets there 30 times per second; with the parity
//     section of this patch it gets there 60. Measured with a hook on
//     the call site: 6 thinks per second per brain stock, 12 patched.
//
//     That is what makes NPCs look around twice as much: how often they
//     look, how fast they turn and how long they keep looking are the
//     same fact. Measured without a threshold (total angular path over
//     time): stock 9.33 deg/s, without this section +73%, with it 9.49.
//
//     It is a threshold counted in ticks, window-shaped, so it doubles
//     with 2N: M from 5 to 10. BOTH sites carry the same 5 -- one is
//     the phase divisor and the other the index divisor -- and moving
//     only one misaligns the groups.
//
//       0x00293B6C  addiu $v0, $zero, 5  ->  10   (phase)
//       0x00293B9C  addiu $s2, $zero, 5  ->  10   (index)
//
patch=1,EE,00293B6C,word,2402000A
patch=1,EE,00293B9C,word,2412000A

// 15. blinkrate -- the HUD blinks. 0x001AF010 runs once per PRESENTED
//     frame (measured, 1.000 per frame): it is the HUD, hung off the
//     video frame and not off the world update, so here the cause is
//     the flip divider itself.
//
//     Inside there are two adjacent byte counters, mutually exclusive,
//     chosen by a flag:
//
//       [0x0050D538]  the "Hold Up" order        2013 ms -> 1001
//       [0x0050D539]  minimap objective          8542 ms -> 4275
//
//     Doubling the thresholds is NOT enough: the first one restarts
//     when it reaches 60 and is fixed with 120, but the second one
//     NEVER restarts -- at 90 it clears the message and keeps
//     counting, and what closes its cycle is the sb that truncates it
//     to a byte. Its real period is 256 frames, and doubling the 90
//     fixes the lit phase and breaks the dark one (measured: 2135 ms
//     against stock's 4694).
//
//     What works for both is advancing the counter EVERY OTHER frame.
//     These are counted things: what keeps its wall-clock duration is
//     the count, not an edge, so gating on parity is correct here.
//
//     The hook sits on the read of the flag that picks the branch, and
//     the routine skips both branches entirely on odd frames, landing
//     where they converge. Result: 2.00 frames per increment, cycle A
//     2006 ms and cycle B 8542 ms, both identical to stock.
//
patch=1,EE,000F2000,word,3C020051
patch=1,EE,000F2004,word,8C42C654
patch=1,EE,000F2008,word,30420001
patch=1,EE,000F200C,word,14400002
patch=1,EE,000F2010,word,84620032
patch=1,EE,000F2014,word,0806C43A
patch=1,EE,000F2018,word,00000000
patch=1,EE,000F201C,word,0806C452
patch=1,EE,000F2020,word,00000000
patch=1,EE,001B10E4,word,0803C800

// 16. wheelrate -- the selected icon of the COMMAND WHEEL. The widget
//     lives at [[0x00600840 + idx*4] + 8] + 0x1EF0 (HUDShowWarCommand
//     turns it on) and inside it [widget+0x1578] rises +1 PER FRAME.
//     0x001A7D34 compares it against thresholds to decide what to draw,
//     and BOTH parts -- the yellow highlight and the hand -- come from
//     the same counter against different thresholds: that is why the
//     highlight goes off before the hand, in stock too.
//
//     At 60 fps the counter runs at double speed and the icon blinks at
//     double speed. The fix is half cadence, like brainrate and
//     blinkrate: the counter advances every other frame. The hook sits
//     on the load and the routine skips the addiu on odd frames,
//     landing on the jal, whose delay slot rewrites the unchanged value.
//
patch=1,EE,000F2080,word,3C010051
patch=1,EE,000F2084,word,8C21C654
patch=1,EE,000F2088,word,30210001
patch=1,EE,000F208C,word,14200003
patch=1,EE,000F2090,word,8E821578
patch=1,EE,000F2094,word,0806A0DF
patch=1,EE,000F2098,word,00000000
patch=1,EE,000F209C,word,0806A0E0
patch=1,EE,000F20A0,word,00000000
patch=1,EE,001A8374,word,0803C820

// 17. radarflash -- the objective W on the MINIMAP (not the physical
//     world marker, which is fine). The radar item is a HANDLE, not a
//     pointer: index in the high halfword, generation in the low one,
//     against the entity registry at 0x006EBD38. Resolved, the object
//     holds at +0xB0 the color with alpha, which alternates between
//     0xFFFFFFFF and 0xFFFFFF00.
//
//     ONE counter drives both the frequency AND the duration, which is
//     why the two symptoms come together -- it blinks faster and stops
//     blinking sooner:
//
//       0x001C4F44  addiu $a0, $zero, 100   starts at 100
//       0x001C5774  beq   $v0, $zero, end   ends at 0     <- DURATION
//       0x001C5778  andi  $v0, $v0, 3       every 4       <- FREQUENCY
//       0x001C57E4  addiu $v0, $v0, -1      drops 1 PER FRAME
//
//     No half cadence here: the counter is a byte and 100 -> 200 just
//     fits, so doubling both numbers is simpler and needs neither cave
//     nor hook. Mind the width of the type: 400 does not fit.
//
//     Measured with the patch active from frame 0: stock blinks for
//     3971 ms with a 133 ms half period, the unfixed patch 2269 / 67,
//     and with this section 3937 / 133.
//
patch=1,EE,001C4F44,word,240400C8
patch=1,EE,001C5778,word,30420007

// 18. swayrate + swayamp -- the sprint CAMERA SWAY. The generator is a
//     triangle wave with a random step: every tick it adds
//     sign * RandomRange(0.125,0.25) * K to the accumulator at +0x3F8
//     of the camera object, and when it passes |K| it clamps it and
//     flips the sign at +0x3F4. The step is added PER TICK, without dt.
//
//     Measured on a straight street with L2 and the stick held: the
//     half period is 11 ticks in both phases, 367 ms against 184. And
//     the constants predict it without measuring:
//     2K/(0.1875*K) = 10.7 ticks.
//
//     swayrate -- the FREQUENCY. Half step on the limits of the random
//     range. K is not touched, since it is both the scale of the step
//     and the clamp: lowering it would shrink the amplitude and leave
//     the frequency where it is. The half period in ticks is
//     2/mean_rand, so it does not depend on K, which is why the two
//     knobs do not interfere.
//
//     swayamp -- the AMPLITUDE. The TWO consumers of the accumulator
//     have different gains at 60 fps: the triangle at 0x00133714
//     (sp[432] += unit * acc, the fundamental) comes out x2.58 and the
//     fold at 0x0013373C (sp[440] += (|acc|*2-K)*0.6, which by taking
//     |acc| gives the 2nd harmonic) comes out x1.78. A single K cannot
//     handle both, so there is one knob per path: K at 3/8 puts the
//     fundamental at 0.97 and the 0.6 at 0.9 brings the fold back to
//     1.00. The -K goes together with the K: it is what centers the
//     remapping of |acc|.
//
//     Against stock, A/B/A/B/A, reproducing to the fourth decimal on
//     both passes: frequency 1.33 Hz against 1.32 (x1.01) and rms
//     amplitude 0.517 against 0.520 (x0.99), with each band within 4%.
//     Without these sections: 2.66 Hz (x2.02) and 0.869 (x1.67).
//
//     This does NOT cover the WALKING sway (no L2): there the generator
//     is off -- +0x3F8 is exactly 0 -- and the footstep sway is another
//     mechanism, at 3.0 Hz, which section 19 handles.
//
patch=1,EE,001336B8,word,3C013D80
patch=1,EE,001336C0,word,3C013E00
patch=1,EE,001336D0,word,3C013C2C
patch=1,EE,001336D4,word,34210831
patch=1,EE,00133750,word,3C01BC2C
patch=1,EE,00133754,word,34210831
patch=1,EE,00133760,word,3C013F66
patch=1,EE,00133764,word,34216666

// 19. aimlp32x59 -- the CAMERA SWAY while WALKING, WITHOUT shifting the
//     framing.
//
//     The camera's orientation is exactly look_at([cam+0x240]) from
//     [cam+0x10]: all of the sway goes through that point. In stock it
//     is damped by a LENGTH CLAMP that catches it at 0.629 from the
//     target and re-anchors it at 0.365 EVERY frame; at 60 fps the
//     filter converges faster, the point never reaches the design
//     offset and the clamp runs ZERO times.
//
//     Shrinking that re-anchoring radius reproduces the stock sway but
//     SHIFTS THE FRAMING by up to 1.92 degrees with the camera tilted,
//     and not through a calibration error: the radius IS the lever arm
//     of the damping, so no knob at that site gives both things.
//
//     aimlp32x59 damps THE DIRECTION and nothing else: a one-pole
//     low-pass on w = aim - P0, with one alpha per axis (0.32 in xy,
//     which is the yaw, and 0.59 in z, the pitch), and then
//     aim = P0 + w_s. It is NEUTRAL AT STEADY STATE by construction --
//     with a constant w it returns w -- so it cannot shift the framing:
//     measured standing still, 0.00000 degrees with any alpha. The
//     radius stays where the game leaves it and the clamp keeps
//     control.
//
//     A ONE-word hook at 0x00134280, the JOINT through which the three
//     branches that write [cam+0x240] pass; $at/$v0/$v1/$a0 are dead
//     there and the cave works entirely in COP2, so nothing needs
//     saving. Limited to [cam+0x3E0] == 0: the sprint is covered by
//     swayrate/swayamp.
//
//     Measured, reproduced in two runs: walking, yaw x0.99 and pitch
//     x1.01 against stock, and framing 0.00000 degrees. Sweep of the
//     knob: 0.50/0.50 -> x1.47/x0.81, 0.25/0.25 -> x0.80/x0.41,
//     0.32/0.65 -> x1.00/x1.09, 0.32/0.59 -> x0.99/x1.01.
//
//     The alphas live in the scratch area and ARE emitted (they are
//     constants); w_prev is NOT emitted, the routine writes it. On a
//     cold start it begins at zero, the best constant seed possible:
//     |w| is always 0.365, so the first frame's error is exactly 0.365
//     and any other constant of that magnitude can give up to twice as
//     much. It decays in ~12 frames and happens once per cold start.
//
//     The routine lives at 0x000F5800 and also removes three jolts a
//     plain filter would add: when picking up a hat or a weapon (the
//     pivot height changes for a while), when moving forward (the
//     persistent height moves) and when leaving a sprint (the history
//     would stay frozen). The Z of w_prev comes from the game's
//     previous aim point; during a sprint the history is refreshed
//     without touching the aim.
//
patch=1,EE,000F5800,word,3C01000F
patch=1,EE,000F5804,word,34211EF0
patch=1,EE,000F5808,word,7AA20240
patch=1,EE,000F580C,word,7AA30180
patch=1,EE,000F5810,word,48A20800
patch=1,EE,000F5814,word,48A31000
patch=1,EE,000F5818,word,4BC208EC
