// The substep constants at 0x0030C8CC (1/60) and 0x0030C924 (1/120)
// are not touched here. They belong to the boss's knife slow motion,
// which section 64 (knifeslow) handles.


// 23. camdrag -- the CAMERA DIP WHILE JOGGING.
//
//     Running without L2, stock lowers the camera 0.162 units over the
//     point it aims at and the unfixed patch only 0.028: the camera
//     sits higher and flatter. Standing and walking are IDENTICAL to
//     four decimals in both configurations, so the whole defect lives
//     in a single gait.
//
//     The model, measured: [cam+0x250] is the RESTING POSE (height
//     1.3394 and distance 3.50 over the target, equal in all four
//     conditions), the camera DRAGS horizontally behind it, and
//     |cam - P0| has a CAP of 3.85. Below the cap the height does not
//     change and only the distance grows; past the cap the camera goes
//     down along the sphere, and THAT is the dip. The height was never
//     a variable: it is what the arm has left.
//
//     The drag is a lerp PER TICK without dt, and the giveaway is that
//     walking it is worth 3.62 ticks in stock and 3.60 patched -- the
//     same lag in TICKS, so half in seconds. Jogging this does not hold
//     because there it saturates against the cap, which is why the dip
//     ratio is 0.17 and not 0.5.
//
//     The gain is the 0.22 at 0x00132A80, which ends up in
//     [cam+0x388]. Found by bisecting 23 suspect constants in the
//     camera code: this one alone reproduces the whole effect.
//
//     The new value converts the half-life to the doubled rate,
//     1 - sqrt(1 - 0.22) = 0.116824, and a blind sweep confirmed it:
//     poking [cam+0x388] the sweep gave 0.1170.
//
//     Measured: jogging 1.3120 -> 1.1845 against stock's 1.1797 (96%
//     of the defect), and the horizontal drag 3.6643 against 3.6648.
//     Standing and walking do not move.
//
patch=1,EE,00132A80,word,3C013DEF
patch=1,EE,00132A84,word,34214160


// 24. aimturn -- the AIMING SPEED with throwable objects.
//
//     Aiming with a bottle (L1 held and the left stick) the reticle
//     crosses the screen at exactly 2.00x: 103.2 degrees/s in stock
//     against 206.4, on both axes.
//
//     The site is 0x00244770, the AIM state handler: the game installs
//     it in [human+0x1C0] while L1 is held and removes it on release
//     (without L1 there is another one, 0x00240E38). Inside, the turn
//     is
//
//         angle = stick_bytes * 0.0005890487 rad     <- PER TICK
//         q = q * quat(angle)
//
//     and the vertical axis does the same on the scalar [human+0x630],
//     clamped to +-0.62832 rad (36 degrees). There is NO dt in either,
//     so at 60 fps it turns twice as far per second.
//
//     Measured stick byte by stick byte, the turn is exactly
//     0.03375 degrees * (byte - 153): linear, with the dead zone at 153
//     and 3.4425 degrees per tick at full deflection. That slope is
//     what identified the constant -- the full-deflection step does not
//     exist as a literal in the ELF, the step PER BYTE does.
//
//     The fix is half a step, the same shape as lockrate and swayrate:
//     the same float divided by two, one word per axis. Measured:
//     103.2 degrees/s against stock's 103.2, reproduced over two
//     A/B/C passes.
//
patch=1,EE,00244D60,word,3C01399A
patch=1,EE,00244DE4,word,3C01399A

// 25. mashhold -- HOW LONG each press of the UNCUFF has to last.
//
//     The minigame does not count edges: it counts the HELD action the
//     pad classifier publishes, and the script sets the two it accepts
//     ([0x00510980] = 6 = L1 and [0x00510984] = 4 = R1). The walker at
//     0x00147940 publishes ONE action per tick, the one from the last
//     match, and two of its tables name the same button:
//
//         0x005DDD10 -> 0x00144B88   state 6   hist[cursor] & L1
//         0x005DDDD0 -> 0x00144BF0   state 7   edge: and not at cursor-1
//
//     The second one is walked AFTER, so on the edge frame 7 wins and 6
//     only comes out on the next sample: the button has to be down in
//     TWO consecutive samples of the ring. The ring advances one sample
//     per tick (measured: 120 advances in 120 frames), so 33 ms in
//     stock and 17 at 60 fps.
//
//     Measured with a ladder of durations, the shortest press the
//     minigame counts: stock 67 ms, 60 fps 33 -- the bare 60 fps base
//     already gives 33, no later section causes it.
//
//     This does NOT touch the classifier, which half the game uses: it
//     goes on the only static caller of the "was there a press"
//     routine (0x0027BCD8) and adds the condition that came for free at
//     30 Hz -- that the button was also down THREE samples back. If it
//     does not hold it returns 0 WITHOUT calling the original, so the
//     alternation is not disturbed: just as when stock does not see the
//     press.
//
//     The hook lands on a `jal` and so it jumps with `jal`: the
//     original routine returns to the caller by itself through $ra.
//
//     Calibrated against stock, presses the minigame counts at 8 Hz:
//
//         press          33   42   50   67  100 ms
//         stock          no   no   no   yes  yes
//         without this   YES  YES  yes  yes  yes
//         with N=3       no   no   no   yes  yes
//
//     N=4 never counts and N=2 falls short: the 3 comes from the
//     calibration, not from reasoning.
//
patch=1,EE,000E2180,word,8C820020
patch=1,EE,000E2184,word,3C010051
patch=1,EE,000E2188,word,8C230980
patch=1,EE,000E218C,word,00000000
patch=1,EE,000E2190,word,10430005
