// 30. climbphase -- the SUNKEN FRAME WHEN CLIMBING.
//
//     The symptom: when the SECOND PART of a climb starts, the body
//     shows up shifted downwards for one frame and comes back on the
//     next. It happens with the player and with both of Vermin's climbs
//     in the mini cutscene.
//
//     The cause is AN INTERPOLATION BETWEEN TWO KEYS EXPRESSED ON THE
//     TWO SIDES OF A RELOCATION OF THE ACTOR. The clip carries a 0x40
//     event that changes the actor's POSITION, and the pose translation
//     includes the compensating jump that cancels it. At 30 fps both
//     land on the same instant; at 60 the engine interpolates the
//     compensation BEFORE running the event, so during the extra half
//     frame part of the compensation is applied without the character
//     having been relocated yet. That sinks it and shifts it sideways.
//
//     Measured inside the computation (stick up + Triangle):
//
//       field   clip t       cursor   pose Z      physical Z
//       14      0            0        1.2179      0.2231
//       15      0.375        0        0.8812      0.2231   <- event pending
//       16      0.75         1        0.3087      1.2580   <- already run
//
//     The correction does NOT touch the animation clock or bring the
//     event forward: it runs the original decoder first and, if there
//     is a relocation pending right at the end of the interval, gives
//     back to XYZ the fraction that should not be compensated yet:
//
//       pose += alpha * event_displacement
//
//     alpha is NOT recomputed: the decoder itself leaves it in $f12
//     (sub.s $f12,$f12,$f3 at 0x00104C2C and div.s $f12,$f12,$f2 at
//     0x00104C60, and nothing else writes it until it returns), so this
//     uses the same one the engine used and cannot get out of sync.
//
//     The site is 0x00104EC0, the jal to the TRANSLATION decoder
//     (0x00104BC8, the same one cutshift calls from elsewhere) for the
//     root channel. The caller passes it $a0 = [s4] the key,
//     $a1 = s0+0x10 the destination and $a2 = [s5] the cursor.
//
//     The delay slot (0x00104EC4, mov.s $f12, $f21) is NOT touched and
//     so it does NOT appear below: it runs anyway before the jump -- it
//     is the delay slot of a jal -- and it is the time the routine
//     hands to the decoder.
//
//     Five guards, and none of them names the climb, the player or a
//     clip:
//
//       1. [0x005102CC] == 1/60. At 30 fps the routine is INERT, and
//          that was measured: 59 captures and 65 physical positions
//          identical to stock;
//       2. [anim+0x1E] & 0x8010 == 0: events are not disabled;
//       3. u16[anim+0x124] < u16[anim+0x126]: an event is left to
//          process;
//       4. [anim+0x120] non-null and u16[event+2] == 0x40, the type
//          "relocate the actor";
//       5. u16[event] - u16[anim+0x128] == u8[key+8] + cursor: the
//          relocation lands EXACTLY at the end of this interval. This
//          is the one that keeps the routine from touching any other
//          interpolation.
//
//     The displacement is three floats at event+8/+12/+16. The
//     corrected branch reserves 32 bytes, keeps $ra and the event
//     pointer, calls the decoder and returns with $sp restored; the one
//     that fails the guards JUMPS to the decoder keeping the $ra the
//     hook created.
//
//     Measured, excursion of the color centroid in the visible frame:
//
//       player                    38.0844 px  ->  7.1761
//       Vermin, first climb       21.2290     ->  1.8474
//       Vermin, second climb      32.6166     ->  0.6265
//
//     and ONLY THOSE THREE FRAMES CHANGE out of the 156 captures
//     compared. The player's 65 physical positions and the 370 records
//     of the 12 transforms of the mini cutscene are identical to the
//     patch without this section: it changes what is DRAWN on that
//     half frame, not where anyone is.
//
//     The routine, disassembled:
//
//       0x000E2B00  3C020051  lui   $v0, 0x0051
//       0x000E2B04  344202CC  ori   $v0, $v0, 716
//       0x000E2B08  8C420000  lw    $v0, 0($v0)
//       0x000E2B0C  3C033C88  lui   $v1, 0x3C88
//       0x000E2B10  34638889  ori   $v1, $v1, -30583
//       0x000E2B14  14430030  bne   $v0, $v1, 0x000E2BD8
//       0x000E2B18  00000000  nop
//       0x000E2B1C  9662001E  lhu   $v0, 30($s3)
//       0x000E2B20  30428010  andi  $v0, $v0, -32752
//       0x000E2B24  1440002C  bne   $v0, $zero, 0x000E2BD8
//       0x000E2B28  00000000  nop
//       0x000E2B2C  96620124  lhu   $v0, 292($s3)
//       0x000E2B30  96630126  lhu   $v1, 294($s3)
