// 49. inputlag -- STOCK input response, switchable in game.
//     At 60 fps the visible response to a press came sooner than in
//     stock, because the delays the engine counts in updates last half
//     as long. This section compensates with a queue of six complete
//     pad samples before they are classified, about 100 ms.
//
//     By default -- RAM at zero -- the response is STOCK's. Holding
//     Select + L2 + R2 for ONE SECOND switches it to OFF (no queue, the
//     fastest response) and back. 'Input lag: STOCK' / 'Input lag: OFF'
//     shows at the top center for 2.5 s, drawn with the HUD's text
//     routine after the interface: it uses none of the game's texts.
//     The mode and the queues live at 0x000D9000..0x000D9380 and are
//     NOT written here: a save state carries the mode it had.
//
//     The queue covers the controllers on BOTH ports (physical pad
//     indices 0 and 4, queues 0 and 1).
//
//     And it is applied PER ACTION: the queue compensates the delay
//     stock has on the common punch, but the grapple attack and the
//     knife throw do not have it and would come out ~100 ms late. On
//     leaving the queue, a wrapper checks whether a Square or X press
//     STARTS with a knife in hand or while grabbing, unarmed, someone
//     who is grabbing back: those bits come out with ONE sample of delay
//     and stay owned until released (six tags parallel to the queue
//     drop their delayed copy). The rest of the pad, with all six. Only
//     in STOCK mode.
//
//     The button OWNERSHIP (0x000B5800, 0x100 per controller) is zeroed
//     ONCE at boot and NEVER with patch=1. Zero means 'no history': the
//     first sample starts it.
patch=0,EE,000B5800,word,00000000
patch=0,EE,000B5804,word,00000000
patch=0,EE,000B5808,word,00000000
patch=0,EE,000B580C,word,00000000
